Data Files (finland_scraping/)
finland_metadata.csv
The master file capturing the legal status of Finnish labor contracts.

Linking Metadata to Files and Assigning Document Dates

Each row in `finland_metadata.csv` matches text and PDF assets via the columns `PDF_1` through `PDF_7`. These columns list separate documents belonging to the same long-term agreement sequence across different renewal years.

Binary PDFs and extracted text files share identical base names, differing only by directory and file extension:
Source Binary: `finland_pdfs/[Filename].pdf`
Raw Text (OCR): `txts/[Filename].txt`
English Translation:`translated_txts/[Filename].txt`

Determining Dates for Multi-Year PDFs
Because a single row contains multiple historical versions of an agreement, individual document dates are assigned chronologically using filename tokens:
The Filename Year Token:Filenames follow a `[Name][YY][MM].pdf` convention. For example, `Apteek2505.pdf` indicates an asset from May 2025, while `Apteek2305.pdf` indicates May 2023.
The Latest Document: The file featuring the highest year token matches the row's official `Date of entry into force...` and `Expiry date...` values.

Year: The year of the official registry entry.
Date of Issue: The date the Committee for Confirming General Applicability issued its decision.
Date of signature of collective agreement: The date the actual labor contract was signed.
Date of entry into force...: When the agreement began to apply.
Expiry date...: When the current coverage cycle ends.

General applicability: Legal status (e.g., allmänt bindande - generally binding).

Note: There are only ~200 rows of metadata because Finland has sector-level agreements. Most
of these agreements have multiple pdfs corresponding to different versions of the agreement.

Scripts & Replication Workflow
1. A-Z Index Crawler (finland_scraper.ipynb)
This script harvests data from the Finlex authorities' database.

Technique: Scans the alphabetical index (A-Z) of collective agreements. It uses Selenium to handle paginated results (scanning multiple pages per letter) to harvest all Source_URL entries.


2. Finnish Text Extraction (finland_extractor.ipynb)
A specialized extraction tool designed for Finnish characters.

Logic: Converts PDF pages to high-resolution (300 DPI) images using pdf2image.

OCR Implementation: Uses pytesseract with the lang='fin' model. This is critical for capturing Finnish-specific characters (ä, ö) and specialized legal vocabulary accurately.

Output: Generates standardized .txt files for every agreement in the archive.

3. Automated Translation (finland_translator.ipynb)
Handles the conversion of raw Finnish text into English research materials.

Logic: Uses the googletrans API. It includes a specific clean_text function to strip "toxic" symbols (like strings of 'o' or '.' used in Finnish tables of contents (side effect of OCR)) that commonly cause API errors.

Resilience: Implements a 2-second "human delay" between chunks and manages long files by splitting them into 2,000-character segments.

4. Descriptive Analysis (finland_descriptives.ipynb)
Generates visualizations.

Date Handling: Uses a strict Finnish dot-notation parser (%d.%m.%Y). Handles the specific "Date of Issue" vs. "Date of Signature" logic explored in the report.

Active Agreement Logic: Employs Overlap Logic. An agreement is counted as active for a year if its implementation period (date_effective to date_expiry) overlaps with that calendar year for at least one day.

Exclusion: Explicitly filters out 2026 data.

Software Requirements
Python 3.12

System Dependencies: Requires Tesseract OCR (with Finnish language pack) and Poppler (for PDF-to-image conversion).

Core Packages: pandas, selenium, matplotlib, pytesseract, pdf2image, googletrans.